@ ======== make and unmake ========
@ ---- PT holds white's values only: the row t + PT (t = 1-6), the column the square s (the empty piece: the row PT,
@ zeros). The piece-square values are the same on both wings, so black's table is white's turned round (the square
@ s seen from black is 121 - s: a1 22 <-> h8 99) and the value of a black piece is minus white's there:
@ value(-t, s) = -value(t, 121 - s). The column of a piece of the colour c: 60.5 + c (s - 60.5).
@ The macro :PTW: Y = a square s, X = a piece p of the colour c = R45 (or 0): X = c x its value there, its own
@ (white's) table value W(|p|, its square) ----
@ ---- the macro :PTX: Y = a square s, X = a piece p of the other colour -R45 (or 0): X = -c x its value there (its own
@ table value: the row |p| + PT = PT - c p, the column 60.5 - c (s - 60.5)) ----
@ ---- LBL :VAL: Y = a square, X = a piece of the colour R45: X = its signed value there (the macro :PTW:) ----
